  "narrative": {
    "intro": "The Department of Agriculture obligated $7B in contracts in FY2026 to date. The Department of Agriculture issued 19,672 awards with an average award size of $355K, and obligations fell 27.1% year over year. Obligations peaked within this series at $11.6B in FY24, then fell to $9.6B in FY25 and to $7B in FY26. Nonscheduled Chartered Freight Air Transportation is the single largest NAICS category in FY2026 to date at $735M, followed by Highway, Street, and Bridge Construction at $448M and Animal (except Poultry) Slaughtering at $425M. The Department of Agriculture also shows large FY2026 to date spending in food manufacturing categories such as Fruit and Vegetable Canning at $397M, Poultry Processing at $395M, and Cheese Manufacturing at $336M. KIEWIT INFRASTRUCTURE SOUTH CO is the top vendor in FY2026 to date at $261M, followed by TYSON FOODS INC. at $176M and PALANTIR TECHNOLOGIES INC. at $129M. The figures are fiscal year to date and represent contract obligations only.",
    "sections": [
      {
        "heading": "How has Agriculture's total contracting trended recently?",
        "body": "The Department of Agriculture obligated $9.7B in FY21, $10B in FY22, $11.5B in FY23, $11.6B in FY24, $9.6B in FY25, and $7B in FY26 to date. The Department of Agriculture recorded a year-over-year decline of down 27.1% in FY2026 to date compared with the prior year."
      },
      {
        "heading": "What are the biggest contract categories and vendors right now?",
        "body": "Nonscheduled Chartered Freight Air Transportation leads NAICS categories in FY2026 to date at $735M, followed by Highway, Street, and Bridge Construction at $448M and Animal (except Poultry) Slaughtering at $425M. KIEWIT INFRASTRUCTURE SOUTH CO is the largest vendor in FY2026 to date at $261M, with TYSON FOODS INC. at $176M and PALANTIR TECHNOLOGIES INC. at $129M rounding out the top three."
      }
    ]
  },
  "faq": [
    {
      "q": "How much does Department of Agriculture spend on contracts?",
      "a": "The Department of Agriculture has obligated $7B in contracts in FY2026 to date. The Department of Agriculture issued 19,672 awards with an average award size of $355K, and obligations are down 27.1% year over year. Historical obligations were $9.7B in FY21, $10B in FY22, $11.5B in FY23, $11.6B in FY24, and $9.6B in FY25."
    },
    {
      "q": "What does Agriculture buy?",
      "a": "The Department of Agriculture buys a mix of transportation, construction, food processing, forestry support, and IT services. Top NAICS categories in FY2026 to date include Nonscheduled Chartered Freight Air Transportation at $735M, Highway, Street, and Bridge Construction at $448M, Animal (except Poultry) Slaughtering at $425M, Fruit and Vegetable Canning at $397M, and Poultry Processing at $395M."
    },
    {
      "q": "Who are Agriculture's largest contractors?",
      "a": "KIEWIT INFRASTRUCTURE SOUTH CO is the largest contractor to the Department of Agriculture in FY2026 to date at $261M. TYSON FOODS INC. is second at $176M and PALANTIR TECHNOLOGIES INC. is third at $129M. Other top vendors include HELICOPTER TRANSPORT SERVICES, LLC at $116M and PACIFIC COAST PRODUCERS at $109M."
    }
